WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.620
BARRET SCHLOERKE:
OK, so Future's

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:20.140
a wonderful R package
that does a lot of voodoo

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.530
behind the scenes so that
you can easily run code

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:26.550
in a separate R process.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.020
And I think that in itself
deserves a ton of praise,

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:35.300
because the API is so
simple, and is so useful.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.850
I actually don't even
use the shorthand API,

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:43.460
because I like the very
direct, explicit future where

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.710
you say, hey, run this
code in the future,

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.790
and then go get a value.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:52.170
And I think that in itself
is a wonderful handshake,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:57.231
and I really like this and I
really enjoy embracing this.

00:00:57.231 --> 00:01:00.050
This is very useful if
you have, like, hey,

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.560
I want to run 100 simulations,
and I have 10 cores.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.070
Let's make a plan
to use all 10 cores

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.530
and just churn over those.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:14.010
And we can actually use R
on all separate 10 cores,

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.200
or in 10 different R processes.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.500
Hopefully your machine is smart
enough to use all the cores.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.085
But typically R is single--

00:01:23.085 --> 00:01:26.770
single worker is the way
I want to think of it.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:31.130
And so if it's a single worker,
you churn through it serially.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.780
But if we are able
to use future,

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.590
we can now churn through it in
parallel, using as many workers

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.280
as we have available.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.590
And I think that's awesome.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:43.430
It works out really
well, and we can actually

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.290
take a quick peek at this.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.970
So we library(future),
and then we

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:50.090
can set up plan and
say multisession.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:53.360
It's just a good
default. Then we

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.520
can say workers is two
for the demo purposes.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.370
Or for the other example,
I could set it to be 10.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.560
I know that works
great as well-- or 100.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.540
It doesn't matter.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.450
But two works.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.800
And then here, I have my
simulations from 1 to 10

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.280
that I want to run, and I'm
going to create the future,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.590
I'm going to set up the future.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.960
I'm going to say it was
created, and then I'm

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.980
going to return
that future object.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.620
And then finally,
at the end, we will

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.240
look at all of the values
for each of the vals.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.690
So let's library plan, run vals.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.870
And it immediately goes
into-- the first two

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.130
are created right away.

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.980
And then we start to go through
this just a little bit slower,

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.650
but in pairs of twos.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.750
And that's really neat.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.060
If we are looking at
it with a Sys.sleep(2),

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.790
that should have taken
what, 20 seconds?

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:59.510
But if we do a start is
Sys.time, and end Sys.time--

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:05.660
what is it-- end minus
start, maybe this will work.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:06.320
Try it again.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.310
So it took 10 seconds.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:19.040
And this is awesome,
we have two workers,

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.750
and-- oh, it took nine seconds.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.180
Not really 10, but
we have two workers,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.900
and it all finished up
very quickly in parallel.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.930
Not 20 seconds, that's
the important part.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.020
We can get in later to where
that extra second went,

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:34.250
but I'm not too worried.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:37.680
It's roughly 10, so
that's the good part.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.510
And if we get the
values, we have 1 to 10.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.360
Perfect.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.320
This is really neat, but one of
the things that was interesting

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.580
is, when you noticed-- when
you watched how it was working,

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.900
I could not actually
execute this end.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.530
Or this end minus
start until the lapply

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.900
had finished processing.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:05.140
That's little weird, because
I'm saying, hey, dear future,

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.050
please run this in the future.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.200
And this is because it
actually had ran out

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.990
of workers to submit jobs to.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:20.149
So if I switch this, and I
say workers is 100, plan.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:28.180
I start, do the vals, end, it
should go much, much faster.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.980
Oh, it-- let's
actually cancel that.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.670
Let's say workers is 10,
because it's actually

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.750
making a hundred R sessions.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.360
We don't want to do that.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.680
So let's set it up to be 10.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.220
So now they're
created very quickly.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.460
And so let's actually
just redo the whole thing,

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.770
because I didn't even
hit the end time point.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.590
So it took two seconds.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.220
Submitting the jobs
is not trivial.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:00.470
It takes time, but
it's a lot faster

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:03.860
than if we are to
do this in serial.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.750
So, that's good.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.250
And we can look at the values.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.680
Everything is great.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.720
So that's a lot of fun.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:18.380
It's really neat how all
of that is able to be done.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.090
But there's that
funny situation of,

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.970
when you run out of
workers, and what happens

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.170
when we run out of workers.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.410
And my thought is it just
immediately returned.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.690
And turns out, it
actually blocks.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.270
Blocking has very
big implications

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.170
for Shiny and for Plumber.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:39.280
Shiny will actually
treat future processes

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.490
very similar to a promise.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:46.960
And a promise is
a thing in R that

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.060
says you can chain
these promises,

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.490
but you can also
kind of interleave

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:53.470
the processing of promises.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.520
Promises kind of
jump into later.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.810
But the idea is that
you can interleave

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:00.200
the processing of promises.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.520
So if you have a job that takes
a very long amount of time,

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.440
you can actually break
it up into a chain

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:06.970
of multiple promises.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:11.510
And that would allow other
things to work in between,

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.630
rather than waiting for your
job to finish completely.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.420
This idea can also be
scaled up for Shiny

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:23.640
in that, what if we have
multiple users coming in?

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.240
If this user comes in and
says, please process my model,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.660
and it's one big block
of code, no one else

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:31.920
gets to do anything.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.910
All of your cores are
being underutilized.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.020
Nothing.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:40.150
And so instead, you could
use promises to say,

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.150
if you could break up
that section of your model

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:45.420
or processing of that model into
multiple, multiple promises,

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:47.820
then other users
can come in and just

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:49.195
say oh, give me "Hello, world!"

00:06:49.195 --> 00:06:50.820
Give me "Hello,
world!" and no problem.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:54.190
They don't have to
wait till the end.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:56.470
So I think it's really cool.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:07:00.550
Future, we treat just like a
promise, and it gets upgraded,

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.820
and the result is
handled accordingly.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:08.950
The bad part is, future blocks
when submitting a job if there

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.390
is no workers available.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.800
So there is a brand new function
in the promises package called

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.800
future promise.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.880
And a future promise, for all
practical purposes, is just,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.385
I promise to execute
this in the future.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:30.030
So for the majority
of what you'll do,

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.670
whenever you'd normally
say future parentheses,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:37.550
you can kind of replace it with
future promise parentheses.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:41.960
So in our code, if we were
to come in here, and--

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.020
let's actually copy this
and make a new script.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:51.710
And library(promises),
and we'll clear out.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:52.790
our output.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:53.440
Right.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.430
So we say library(future),
library(promises),

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.110
set up our multisession.

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.095
Let's actually adjust our
workers back down to two.

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:05.690
OK.

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:10.850
And then we will adjust our
future to be a future_promise.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.735
And let's try running this.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:26.430
So the time finished right
away, and it actually--

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:30.900
I was able to get control back
to the console right away.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.270
And then these messages
started just showing up

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:36.330
when it started completing
and submitting these jobs.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.400
And I think that's a
very subtle difference,

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.520
but it's a very
important difference.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.530
Because, if I run this again,
of calculating the vals,

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:49.730
I can actually come in
here and say 1 plus 1,

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:52.700
say 2 plus 2, 2 plus 3.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:57.710
And I'm able to submit this work
while waiting for the future

00:08:57.710 --> 00:08:59.360
to finish.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.530
And now my main R
session is not blocked.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:04.770
This is super cool.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.210
This is that "wow"
moment, because now I

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.570
can process other
people's work while not

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:17.530
waiting for that big future job
to finish, or to be submitted.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:21.080
And that is very,
very beneficial.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:25.240
So workers is an
arbitrary thing.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:28.240
You can set it to what you want.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.430
It's dependent on what
you want to use them for.

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:35.770
So if you have things
like submitting data

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:40.180
to my SQL, or not
labor intensive tasks,

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:42.730
but you need to do
a bunch of them,

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:46.180
then maybe you have two to
three of the amount of cores

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:47.830
that you have.

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:50.590
Because they're not going
to be utilizing that much

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:53.260
work, and it's OK.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:55.990
You could even go higher
than that, if it's not

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:58.240
a real intense task.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:02.290
But if you're counting all
the numbers in Fibonacci,

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:06.970
and it's very computation
expensive, then maybe you

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:09.010
do cores minus 1.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:11.090
So if I have eight
cores on my machine,

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:14.740
I might say workers equals 7, so
that I can click on my computer

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:15.850
still.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.850
Otherwise, you'll have all
of your cores maxed out,

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:22.840
and your computer's kind
of rendered useless.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:28.270
But it's up to you as to
how you want to use it.

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:32.230
Workers are not cores
directly, but your computer

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:36.490
will try to offload them and
have them process, because it's

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:38.800
not in the computer's
best interest

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.690
to put them on the same core.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:43.090
So Plumber.

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:47.140
Plumber runs on httpuv,
just like Shiny.

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.290
So a lot of these concepts
were just the same

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:54.430
as if we were doing Plumber
API or a Shiny application.

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:59.170
The concept of working and
using futures and promises

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:01.220
just extends from
one to the other.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.970
So in Plumber,
let's imagine that I

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:07.570
have two functions that I
want to expose in an API.

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:10.300
Plumber is a R package
that will allow you

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:16.460
to create a web API
from R code, just using

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:18.320
a couple of little decorators.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:19.790
In this case, I
have two functions,

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:24.800
the fast calc and slow calc,
and I can expose them as routes

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.090
with the @get/fast/
and @get/slow/.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:32.660
And for me, in the case
of the demo for this,

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:36.050
the id is just representing
the submission order,

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.610
but the calculation
can do whatever

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:40.890
it wants with the
parameters, things like that.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.550
But then that way, when you
see something like slow /1/

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.740
and slow /2/, we
know that slow /2/,

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:49.580
is submitted after slow /1/ even
if they are very close together

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.970
in submission time.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:53.020
Cool.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:56.097
So Plumber handles
this, and in this case,

00:11:56.097 --> 00:11:57.430
we're going to have four routes.

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:01.150
We're going to have a fast
route, a slow, a slow, and then

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:02.500
a fast route.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:06.490
And if we are to process it, all
four submissions kind of done

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.920
at the same time, it
would look like this.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.590
Because R has only one worker.

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:13.865
So we do the fast route,
and then it responds.

00:12:13.865 --> 00:12:15.460
And then OK, it's done.

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:18.070
And then we do a slow route,
and then it processes,

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:22.540
and it takes a very long time.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:26.560
And no one else can do anything,
and slow /3/ is just sitting

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:28.700
there waiting to do work.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:30.650
Then it's done, and
then we respond.

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:31.460
Great.

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:33.740
Slow /3/ comes in, and
it starts to process,

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:35.330
and it is very slow.

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:37.700
And it's taking its time.

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:41.180
And fast /4/ has to wait for
all of this processing time

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.790
to work.

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:44.180
And then OK, we're done.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.660
And then now we can respond,
and then fast /4/ can come

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:49.600
in, and it processes
really quickly.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.660
And then it can
respond, and we're done.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.150
So great, awesome.

00:12:55.150 --> 00:13:00.980
Those four requests came in,
and they took about 20 seconds.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:04.230
If we think that slow takes
10 seconds to calculate,

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.670
and fast is roughly
instantaneous.

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:09.540
So the fast comes in, the
process is right away.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:12.630
Slow comes in, takes
10 seconds to process.

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:15.550
And then slow /3/ had
to wait that whole time,

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:17.190
and then slow /4/
had to wait even--

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:19.320
all 20 seconds for
it to be processed,

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:23.140
even though they kind of
arrived at the same time.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:25.520
This red area is bad.

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.730
This is something
that we can optimize.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:30.880
I can't necessarily make
slow calculation faster,

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:36.340
but I can reduce the waiting
time, and that's a goal.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:40.590
So with Plumber, we can
do this using future.

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:45.680
And this is just talking
about using futures,

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:49.082
and so how they can work
with Plumber and with Shiny.

00:13:49.082 --> 00:13:50.540
A lot of people
don't know of this.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.360
So if you're compiling
a PDF in Shiny,

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:57.920
if you return a future from
your reactive, or your output,

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.760
or an observe, it will
put it in this promise,

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:04.070
and it will listen to it.

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:05.030
It's awesome.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:10.010
I read the docs, but you can
offload things quite well.

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:11.630
The only change
in this situation

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:16.590
is that we're going to wrap our
slow calculation in a future,

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:19.100
and then we will resubmit it.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:23.340
And from before,
it took 20 seconds.

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:27.620
And now it will take 10 seconds.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:32.480
And there's been-- fast /4/ got
reduced by 20 seconds total,

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.560
and slow /3/ did
not have to wait,

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.780
and so it was 10 seconds faster.

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:40.850
So all the wait time in this
example was able to be removed.

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:42.350
We can't reduce the
processing time,

00:14:42.350 --> 00:14:46.250
but we can at least remove
or get rid of the wait time,

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:47.450
and that's awesome.

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.880
And so this is how future
can help you do that.

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:53.440
Cool.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:56.500
So how would this look
like with Plumber?

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.450
Plumber is typically,
or is always,

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.360
in the main R session
or the main worker,

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:02.950
and we can add extra workers.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:05.910
Future worker one,
future worker two.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:08.130
So having those
same routes come in,

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.380
where the slow is actually
wrapped in future,

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:13.470
it will look like so.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:14.070
Oh, yeah.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:17.040
The future worker, we're only
doing it in the process step.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:20.520
So OK, fast comes in, it is
processed really quickly.

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:21.240
Great.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.250
We respond, awesome.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:24.210
Slow comes in.

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:26.340
Hey, it's a future,
let's offload it.

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:26.940
Great.

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:30.060
This allows the main R process
to come in and do the same

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:31.470
thing with slow /3/.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.320
Since we have enough workers
for the jobs, then slow--

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:37.745
or sorry, fast /4/ is able
to process immediately.

00:15:37.745 --> 00:15:39.120
Slow /2/ just
happened to finish,

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:40.440
so that we can send it off.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:44.190
And same with slow /3/.

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:46.610
A lot going on, a
lot of moving parts,

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:51.320
but it's really nice
because it allows

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.480
to offload that slow
processing somewhere else

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.310
to free up that main R worker.

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:56.930
So you can respond
to health checks,

00:15:56.930 --> 00:16:01.820
you can have other users in
Shiny do simple reactivity,

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:03.330
and it's not going
to be a big deal.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:04.788
You're not waiting
for someone else

00:16:04.788 --> 00:16:06.290
to finish running their model.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:11.300
Why should someone else's Shiny
session alter my Shiny session?

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.910
And futures and promises
will help you do that.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:21.335
So that's where we get
that 20 second save,

00:16:21.335 --> 00:16:24.140
by being in parallel.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:25.550
So some limitations.

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:27.930
You were talking about
how many cores do I do?

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:32.480
Well, we saw I was trying to
add 100 cores at one point,

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.130
and my computer
just kind of blew up

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:37.580
a little bit, because it
was spawning 100 R sessions.

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:39.290
Maybe not the best idea.

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:42.080
Two to three is pretty
good, or one to one

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:46.550
if you're computationally
intense preprocessing.

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:48.620
Spawning a future
is not instant, kind

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:50.510
of like a quarter of a second.

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:54.140
But if a quarter of a second
really doesn't mean much to you

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.240
for time, then
future is perfect.

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.330
If a quarter of a second
does mean a lot to you,

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:03.260
maybe don't offload it
to an external R session.

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.290
Because maybe that communication
overhead is too much,

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:08.410
and you're already losing time.

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:11.410
So and that's kind of where it
leads into the processing power

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:13.480
is finite and memory
size is finite.

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:16.400
Maybe if your models take
up 10 gigs of memory,

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:17.950
maybe you only use two workers.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:21.569
It's still better than zero.

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:23.849
What happens when I
run out of workers?

00:17:23.849 --> 00:17:28.020
So let's pretend we
have six slow routes all

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:29.400
being handled by future.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:33.210
And what would happen
if I slow three,

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:36.030
we will run out of workers.

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.580
So slow /1/ comes in,
offloaded worker one.

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:39.150
Great.

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:41.190
Slow /2/ offloaded worker two.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:46.870
Slow /3/ blocking
the main R session.

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:50.050
And the main R session
is still blocked,

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:52.120
because now slow
/4/ is blocking,

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:55.330
slow /5/ is still blocking
the main R session.

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.210
Slow /6/ is still blocking
the main R session,

00:17:58.210 --> 00:17:59.980
because it can't be
offloaded somewhere.

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:01.990
Now it's been offloaded.

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:05.290
Fast /7/ is still blocking
the main R session,

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:06.880
but it's kind of free.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:14.050
And then now we can undo the
result of the future values.

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:19.570
And now the main R session is
unblocked, it's kind of free.

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:20.650
Phew.

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:24.700
Now we can process slow /5/,
and now we can process slow /6/.

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:28.390
But while we are waiting to
offload slow /3/, /4/, /5/,

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.010
and /6/ to offload
the new workers,

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:32.080
the R process was not usable.

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:33.260
The main R process.

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:36.730
So in the case of Plumber,
you cannot do a health check.

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:40.210
In the case of Shiny, I cannot
load up a new R session,

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:41.410
or a new tab.

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:45.350
It's blocked,
nothing can be done.

00:18:45.350 --> 00:18:48.010
And if we are to visualize
this, the processing time

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.010
takes 30 seconds in
total, offloaded in pairs.

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:54.250
And there's actually a new thing
where the result of slow /1/

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:58.120
and slow /2/ do not get
returned until 20 seconds in.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:01.210
That was when the main
R session became free.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:02.870
And slow /3/ and
/4/ had to wait,

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.890
slow /5/ and /6/ had to
wait, fast /7/ had to wait.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:07.930
And that's not good.

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:11.520
We want to try to reduce that
wait time as much as possible.

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:14.220
To add in as well,
this is the area

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:18.090
where there were more future
requests than future workers.

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:22.980
This is also the area where the
R session plus the final end

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.290
is where the R
session is blocked.

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:27.900
The main R session.

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:30.120
That is where
things are unusable,

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:34.830
even if you're only just handing
them off, or you're waiting.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:37.590
This gray area is bad, and
we'd like to minimize it

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:39.780
as much as possible.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:40.950
Cool.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:44.820
So this is where
future promise came in.

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:47.190
Promise that executes
using a future.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.350
If we were to do this
with future promise,

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:54.470
the execution time
would look like this.

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:57.530
The items slow /1/ and slow
/2/ respond immediately.

00:19:57.530 --> 00:20:01.370
Slow /3/ and /4/,
just like were before.

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:05.540
But the big one is fast /7/
is now actually executing

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:07.430
immediately.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:08.900
It is not waiting for anyone.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:14.812
Fun animations, and also,
more fun animations.

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:22.250
The big difference here is
where the gray bars are at.

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:25.820
In this case, the gray bars
are only at the start when

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:28.280
we're resolving the
values for /1/ and /2/,

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:30.380
and resolving the
values for /3/ and /4/.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.380
And also for /5/ and /6/,
can't get rid of those.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:36.120
But in between, completely free.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:38.570
You can run health
check routes on Plumber,

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:40.940
you can start new
sessions in Shiny.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:43.760
You're not blocked,
everything is awesome.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:45.890
Any time you say future
parentheses, just

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:47.480
say future promise.

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:51.050
Solve a lot of issues,
and for the most part,

00:20:51.050 --> 00:20:54.560
as long as everything's
scoped just like it should be,

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:58.250
then everything will
behave as expected.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:00.350
For the most part, it's
just a one to one drop in.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:02.750
For comparison, let's
see how the animation

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:04.130
works for this one.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:06.620
So we have all of our /1/
through /6/ slow routes,

00:21:06.620 --> 00:21:08.420
and then a fast route.

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:10.190
And the addition
here is that we're

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:13.280
going to have this
promise queue of a promise

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:16.410
to compute with future.

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:19.997
So it's a prequeue,
not a post queue.

00:21:19.997 --> 00:21:22.580
So we have this prequeue because
there's no workers available,

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:24.280
so we'll just say I will--

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.140
I promise to do this.

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:30.290
So we can now execute
fast /7/ immediately,

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:33.260
and then slow /1/ and slow /2/
have finished processing after

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:35.130
10 seconds.

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.840
And the order may be
a little fuzzy here,

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:42.460
but for the most part, they
go in and out in pairs.

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:45.115
I just-- you can only
animate so well in Keynote.

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:51.640
Yeah, but throughout
the whole process,

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:56.220
the main R session
is free like 90--

00:21:56.220 --> 00:22:00.770
98% of the time, not
blocked 60% of the--

